[Chapter "Against ...c5"]
[Line "Punishing 4...Nc6"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]

{A natural-looking move loses material. This is the kind of trap the Jobava sets.} 1. d4 {Start with the queen's pawn.} 1... d5 2. Nc3 {The Jobava idea: the knight comes to c3 before the c-pawn moves, ready for Nb5 or e4.} 2... Nf6 3. Bf4 {Develop the bishop outside the pawn chain before e3.} 3... c5 4. e3 {Keep the centre solid.} 4... Nc6 {Natural, but a mistake.} 5. Nb5 {Threatens Nc7+, forking the king and rook.} 5... e5 {Black's best try: block the bishop and counterattack.} 6. Bxe5 {Take the pawn; Black's c7 is still weak.} 6... Nxe5 7. dxe5 {White is a pawn up, the f6 knight is attacked, and Nc7+ is still in the air.} *
